{"sequence":16,"timestamp":"2026-03-30T09:05:19.962637394Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-1-49d3b132","body":"In `spawning()` (shrimp.rs:714-717), `density_factor` is computed via `density_repro_factor()` but is NOT applied to `spawn_rate`. The spawn rate is `base_spawn_rate * reproductive_readiness_index * f_mineral`, so density only affects spawning through the readiness EMA (smoothing=0.1). This means a sudden density increase (e.g., adding 50 shrimp at once) takes many days to suppress spawning. Consider multiplying `spawn_rate` by `density_factor` directly (matching how `f_mineral` is already applied directly), or document this as intentional delayed response. The variable `density_factor` is currently only used for event string formatting, which is misleading.","dedup_key":"dbd982faee585f4f197d89b565cea54cb1b2687aa28867281644e3109fb6522d","run_id":"run-20260330074452","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
{"sequence":17,"timestamp":"2026-03-30T09:05:19.971475064Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-1-c4841f7b","body":"GH/mineral factor (`gh_mineral_factor`) is excluded from `reproductive_readiness_factors()` (shrimp.rs:1438-1466), so `dominant_repro_suppression_label()` can never report mineral deficiency as the dominant suppression factor. GH affects spawning (line 707) and hatch success (line 864) through separate direct multipliers, but the snapshot diagnostic `repro_dominant_suppression` would report 'none' even when low GH is the primary reason breeding fails. Add a 'minerals' entry to the readiness factors array (e.g., `(\"minerals\", gh_mineral_factor(chemistry.gh_d(), params))`) so the observability contract matches the acceptance criterion that 'debug output identifies which factors suppressed reproduction'.","dedup_key":"2f8538b6d74baaf0ed4818d5611223deadc361fdbc42e4a46c30593cefc4b1de","run_id":"run-20260330074452","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
{"sequence":18,"timestamp":"2026-03-30T09:05:19.980325579Z","event_type":"amendment_queued","details":{"amendment_id":"fr-1-1d9eb769","body":"`crates/tank_harness/tests/e2e_habitat_geometry_probes.rs:302-325` mutates `TankState::new()` to use 18 cm vs 55 cm fill heights, then only rewrites a subset of dissolved totals. `TankState::new()` had already sized `state.water` for the original default geometry (`crates/tank_core/src/types/state.rs:63-79`), so untouched pools such as alkalinity, Ca/Mg, chloride, DOC/DON, etc. are left at default-tank totals and end up at different concentrations in the shallow and deep runs. That means `probe_light_depth_shallow_vs_deep_growth` is not isolating depth/light anymore; part of the observed divergence can come from stale chemistry that was never rescaled to the new volume.","dedup_key":"8bd2c58f34b62df299a9983cc2e989ad1703659cdf228d386646b77fec570a42","run_id":"run-20260330074452","source":"workflow_stage","source_stage":"final_review"}}
